# Tutorial 20 — Dialogue Presentation
# Reference: docs/scripting/vns/integration/vns-interop.md
# Read scripts/tutorial/README.md for the complete course and linked references.
@scenario {{SCENARIO_PREFIX}}_tutorial_20
@include {{CHARACTERS_INCLUDE}}
{{BG_DECL}}@label start
[mode dialogue standard]
[set ui.dialogueUi project]
[set ui.textBoxAsset none]
[set ui.continueIndicator spinning_arrow]
[if tutorial_still]
[set ui.reduceMotion true]
[else]
[set ui.reduceMotion false]
[endif]
[if !tutorial_initialized]
[set tutorial_count 0]
[flag tutorial_initialized]
[endif]
[set ui.characterHeightFactor 1.5]
[set ui.characterBaselineY 1.5]
[set character.lavender.imageEffect none]
{{BG_START}}
[textspeed 28]
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: The same story can use an ADV textbox, a full reading panel, or speaker bubbles. These are presentation modes, not separate script languages.
@label lesson_menu
> Read an NVL passage -> nvl
> Try speaker bubbles -> bubbles
> Compose accessible text -> text
> Checkpoint -> checkpoint
> Finish and return to the hub -> return_hub
@label nvl
[mode dialogue nvl]
narrator: A page can give a quiet moment room to breathe.
narrator: NVL presentation collects the passage into a larger reading surface. Test paragraph length and the player's font scale.
Lavender: The story still uses ordinary dialogue nodes. A mode change does not require a new script format.
[mode dialogue standard]
Lavender: Back to the standard textbox. Always restore the intended mode at reusable scene boundaries.
[jump lesson_menu]
@label bubbles
[mode dialogue bubble]
[char lavender bubble center]
Lavender: This bubble belongs to my speaker ID. Placement can be left, center, right, or automatic.
[char lavender bubble_offset 12 -8]
Lavender: Small offsets can help with a pose, but check the smallest window and largest font scale before settling on them.
[char lavender bubble clear]
[mode dialogue standard]
[jump lesson_menu]
@label text
Lavender: Inline bold, italic, color, and animated text can support a line. A voice clip, punctuation, and a pause can do the same job without visual motion.
Lavender: Keep important information in actual text. Test long names, translated strings, line breaks, narration, and choices instead of sizing the UI around one sentence.
Lavender: Exercise: tell the same short scene in all three modes. Restore standard mode before leaving and explain which version best serves the scene.
[jump lesson_menu]
@label checkpoint
Lavender: You launch a standalone lesson after an NVL scene. What keeps its appearance predictable?
> Set its intended presentation mode at entry -> correct
> Assume the previous scene used the same mode -> retry
@label retry
Lavender: Presentation is state. Set the mode you need at a reusable entry point.
[jump checkpoint]
@label correct
Lavender: Exactly. That is why every workshop begins by restoring its dialogue presentation.
[jump return_hub]

@label return_hub
[mode dialogue standard]
[char lavender bubble clear]
[if !tutorial_done_20]
[flag tutorial_done_20]
[inc tutorial_count]
[endif]
[goto {{HUB_TARGET}}:topics_page_4]
